---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { buildGraph } from '../lib/graph.mjs';
const { stats } = buildGraph();
---
<Base title="About" description="How LolChain models exploit chains.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="crumb"><a href="/">LolChain</a> / ABOUT</div>
    <section class="hero" style="padding:30px 0 10px">
      <div class="label kicker">About</div>
      <h1 style="font-size:clamp(28px,4.5vw,44px)">A graph of capability handoffs.</h1>
    </section>

    <article class="prose">
      <p>
        LolChain is a "Living Off the Land" reference — like GTFOBins or lolcreds — but
        for <strong>vulnerability chains</strong> instead of single primitives. It
        currently models <strong>{stats.nodeCount} vulns</strong>,
        <strong>{stats.chainCount} curated chains</strong>, and
        <strong>{stats.derivedCount} derived candidates</strong> across
        {stats.categoryCount} categories.
      </p>

      <h2>The core idea</h2>
      <p>
        Every vuln is a handoff. Each node <strong>requires</strong> some capabilities
        and <strong>provides</strong> others. An edge exists wherever one node's
        <code>provides</code> satisfies another node's <code>requires</code>. That single
        rule powers every query mode and lets new vulns slot in by capability tag, not
        by hand-wired links.
      </p>

      <h3>Capabilities</h3>
      <p>
        The shared vocabulary — the "handoff currency" (e.g. <code>server-side-request</code>,
        <code>os-command-exec</code>, <code>cloud-credentials</code>). Curated and small;
        it is the glue of the graph.
      </p>
      <h3>Goals</h3>
      <p>
        A controlled vocabulary of end states (<code>account-takeover</code>,
        <code>cloud-account-compromise</code>, <code>cluster-admin</code>…). A node
        <code>achieves</code> a goal when reaching it realises that outcome. Goal-directed
        search matches these.
      </p>

      <h2>Two kinds of edge</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Curated</strong> — human-authored handoffs with an outcome,
          difficulty, and references. Shown first and marked <em>documented</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>Derived</strong> — inferred by the build wherever capabilities line up
          and no curated edge exists. Shown as <em>derived · unverified</em>: plausible,
          not confirmed.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Query modes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>I have X</strong> — forward reachability: everything a foothold
          unlocks, hop by hop.</li>
        <li><strong>X → goal Z</strong> — ranked paths to a goal, optionally forced
          through a chosen node.</li>
        <li><strong>I want Z</strong> — backward search: what achieves the goal and what
          each step needs.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Reading a node</h2>
      <p>
        Each node page shows its capability handoff (requires / provides / achieves),
        the chains out of it (curated and derived), what reaches it, and a defense panel
        with detection, remediation, and MITRE ATT&amp;CK mappings.
      </p>

      <h2>Contributing</h2>
      <p>
        Data is YAML in <code>data/nodes/</code>, validated against a JSON Schema. See
        <a href="https://github.com/">the repository</a> and <code>CONTRIBUTING.md</code>
        for the node shape and how to add one. Tiers: <strong>basic</strong>,
        <strong>advanced</strong>, <strong>expert</strong>. Categories: web, cloud,
        kubernetes, cicd, llm, mobile, network.
      </p>

      <p style="margin-top:28px"><a href="/scope/">→ Scope &amp; ethics</a></p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
